OSINT REPORT

Target: owasp.org
Organization: OWASP Foundation
Analyst: Ifeanyi Gabriel Alexander
Date: 26 November 2026

1. Executive Summary

This report contains a basic OSINT investigation of the OWASP website (owasp.org). The aim was to gather information about the domain, its history and publicly available subdomains without directly attacking or testing the website. Public sources such as WHOIS, the Wayback Machine and crt.sh were used as the main sources of information.

2. Introduction

Objective:

The objective of this investigation is to understand how much information about OWASP can be found online using passive information-gathering techniques.

Scope:

The investigation was limited to passive sources only:

WHOIS – to check domain registration information.
Wayback Machine – to check previous versions of the website.
crt.sh – to find subdomains from public certificate records.

Methodology:

The investigation was carried out on 26 November 2026.

The following steps were used:

1. Checked owasp.org using a public WHOIS lookup to find its registration date and registrar.
2. Checked the Wayback Machine to look for older versions of the website.
3. Used crt.sh to search for certificates connected to owasp.org and identify possible subdomains.
4. Reviewed my own public username footprint using my username: [Insert Your Username].

3. Findings

A. Domain Registration

The WHOIS lookup showed the following information:

Domain name: owasp.org
Registrar: GoDaddy.com, LLC
Date registered: 21 September 2001
Expiration date: 21 September 2031
Nameservers: fay.ns.cloudflare.com and west.ns.cloudflare.com

This shows that the domain has been registered for many years and uses Cloudflare nameservers.

B. Archive History

The Wayback Machine was used to check previous versions of owasp.org.

The archive can help show how the website has changed over time, including changes to its design, pages and content.

The archived versions should be compared with the current website to identify any major changes.

C. Subdomains Discovered

crt.sh was used to search public Certificate Transparency records connected to owasp.org.

Some examples of discovered hostnames include:

docs.owasp.org
name-virt-host.owasp.org

These names appeared in publicly available certificate records. However, finding a hostname in certificate records does not automatically mean that the subdomain is currently active.

D. My Own Username Footprint

Username searched: @alexsvntvn

The username was checked using publicly available information to see whether it appeared on websites or online platforms.

Findings: No matching public result was found. 


4. Analysis

The findings show that OWASP has had its domain for a long time and uses Cloudflare nameservers. This may indicate that the organization uses an established domain and cloud-based services to manage parts of its online presence.

The discovered subdomains may indicate that OWASP uses different web services for different purposes, such as documentation and other website functions.

The archive history may also help identify older pages or information that is no longer available on the current website.

However, these findings do not prove that any system is vulnerable. More investigation would be needed before making such a conclusion.

5. Conclusion and Recommendations

This investigation showed that useful information about an organization can be collected without directly accessing or attacking its systems. WHOIS, the Wayback Machine and crt.sh are useful tools for gathering public information.

Two recommendations for defenders:

1. Regularly review public subdomains and remove or secure any old services that are no longer needed.
2. Monitor public certificate records and domain information to identify unexpected changes or certificates.

